<Quota name="Q-PerMinute" type="calendar">
  <DisplayName>Quota per minute per user</DisplayName>
  <!-- StartTime is required for type="calendar" — deployment fails without
       it (InvalidStartTime/omission both reject at build time). GMT, format
       yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss exactly. This never actually got deployed under
       docs/20 (only console steps were completed there), so this
       requirement was never hit until this proxy's first real deploy
       attempt 2026-09-07. The literal date is an arbitrary stable anchor,
       not a meaningful cutover point — calendar quotas only care about the
       time-of-day/interval phase, not the year. -->
  <StartTime>2026-01-01 00:00:00</StartTime>
  <!--
    Interval is a HARDCODED 1 on purpose. It previously read
      ref="verifyapikey.VA-ApiKey.apiproduct.developer.app.attribute.req_per_min"
    which is a malformed mashup of two different variable namespaces
    (apiproduct.<attr> for product attributes, developer.app.attribute.<attr>
    for app attributes) and therefore does not resolve — so the policy fell
    back to the literal 1 and behaved correctly BY ACCIDENT.

    The trap: req_per_min is 60. Anyone "tidying up" that ref into valid
    syntax would make Interval resolve to 60, and with TimeUnit=minute below
    the quota silently becomes 60 requests per SIXTY minutes instead of 60 per
    minute — a 60x tightening that reviews as a cosmetic syntax fix. An
    interval multiplier and a request count are unrelated quantities that
    happen to share an attribute name here; nothing should ever wire one to
    the other.
  -->
  <Interval>1</Interval>
  <TimeUnit>minute</TimeUnit>
  <Allow count="60" countRef="verifyapikey.VA-ApiKey.apiproduct.req_per_min"/>
  <Identifier ref="jwt_oid"/>
  <Distributed>true</Distributed>
  <Synchronous>true</Synchronous>
</Quota>
